build: the upstream catch-up checker, and its self-test

.agent/reference/provenance.md says which upstream file and commit each of ~390
files came from, and Task B12b's job before v0.1.0 is to catch up every row whose
upstream has moved. Described as a manual sweep, that is a sweep nobody does
twice. This does the reading and the `git log`, and leaves the judgement.

What it refuses to do is as load-bearing as what it does. It REPORTS drift and
never fails on it: a nightly that reddens because upstream moved is a nightly
somebody mutes, and then the next drift goes unseen. It fails only when a row is
wrong about its own upstream -- an unparseable line, a synced SHA the checkout
does not have or that is not an ancestor, an upstream path that was not there at
the very commit the row claims to have synced from. And where an upstream checkout
is missing it exits 77 rather than 0, so "I could not look" never reads the same
as "I looked and found nothing".

Two boundaries it keeps deliberately. A file DELETED upstream is drift, not a
malformed row: the row was true when written, and "it is gone now" is exactly the
news this carries. And a row whose CORE-CPP file is gone is reported as stale
without reddening, because check-cmake-hygiene's `provenance` rule already refuses
that in almost the same words -- two gates for one defect gets both ignored, and it
would put this one in the red for any module mid-rewrite, the state it is least
useful in.

The self-test builds throwaway git repositories with real history rather than
stubbing git: the checker is a wrapper around `git log`, `git cat-file` and
`git merge-base`, so a stub would prove only that the stub agrees with itself. Its
fourteen cases were each shown to fail against a checker mutated to get that one
thing wrong -- including the two that assert an exit status of 0, which no
assertion about output would have caught.

Python, and tested in Python: check-cmake-hygiene-selftest.cmake is the shape for
the checkers written IN CMake, and tools/migrate/*_test.py is the precedent for
testing Python here. Building git history from CMake script would be dozens of
execute_process calls doing what subprocess does in one line.

